[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it say more.
We read every body in the export. The ticket holds **three different problems**, and the
queued fix covers only the largest one.

<Callout>
The desk merges on subject alone (<a href="/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject">decision 0001</a>),
so nobody had read these bodies. Reading them is what split the ticket.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Every number above is computed from `data/reports-4471.json`, `data/tenants/` and
`data/desk-notes.json` at render time. The export pages overlap, so rows are deduplicated
by id first.

## The three problems

### `parser-order` — the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first, so dates with a day of 12 or less come back
flipped: *"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July"* (R-41200). A day of 13 or more fails
or lands in the wrong month instead: October bookings shown as January (R-41615). This is
the bug [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) describes, and the queued fix addresses it — parse with
the tenant's locale, not month first.

### `hint-mismatch` — booking-v3's form hints `MM/DD/YYYY`, then reads the field day first

A different bug with the same symptom. Under its date field, booking-v3 prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`; people type what the hint says, and the form then parses their input the
tenant's way, day first. The queued parser fix does **not** touch this: the field needs one
truth, hint and parse agreeing. It reaches only tenants on booking-v3, only through the
form channel — which is why it hid comfortably inside the bigger group.

### `one-day-move` — dates move by one day between viewers

Five reports the desk itself flagged in `desk-notes.json` describe no flip at all: the same
meeting lands on *Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto* (R-41344), an invite dated
before its own booking (R-41236), bookings one day off for one month only (R-41209). That
is a date stored or rendered at a day boundary without its timezone — a different defect
with a different fix, merged here because the subjects matched.

## What we did about it

One ticket per problem, filed where the desk's tracker can see them, each with the count of
reports it covers from the table above:

- [vetcoders/me-gad-ev-3 · issues](https://github.com/vetcoders/me-gad-ev-3/issues) —
  `parser-order`, `hint-mismatch`, `one-day-move`, one issue each.

## Lessons

- A big ticket is *usually* one bug — but "merge on subject, read nothing" guarantees you
  will never notice when it is not. Sampling a handful of bodies per hundred merges would
  have caught all three.
- Intake's tag guesses from the reporter's browser, not the tenant. It disagreed with the
  tenant-derived cause often enough that no triage should trust it alone.
- Counts belong to components. The desk kept working while we wrote this, and the table
  will keep being true after the next export.

